uld then be the number of points in that cluster. But in the given data, c0 has size 7 but 3 exit lambdas, c1 size 4 with 3 exit lambdas, c2 size 2 with 1 exit lambda. So sizes and number of exit lambdas don't match. This suggests that the point_exit_lambdas may be the lambdas at which points exit the cluster, but there may be additional points that are not listed because they go to subclusters? Actually, in condensed tree, the points that go to subclusters are listed as part of the child cluster's exit lambdas. The parent cluster's point_exit_lambdas are only those points that leave the cluster without going into a child (i.e., they exit the tree). That would make sense: c0 has 7 points total; 3 exit directly from c0 (point_exit_lambdas), and the remaining 4 points go to its child clusters (c1 and c2). c1 has 4 points; 3 exit from c1, and 1 goes to child? But c1 has no children (parent c0, no children indicated). c2 size 2, 1 exit, 1 perhaps goes to? Maybe the point_exit_lambdas represent the lambdas at which points exit the cluster (i.e., they leave the hierarchy). The cluster's total points = size. The children inherit some of these points.\n\nGiven the complexity and the fact that the problem statement says \"sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points\", and the input has point_exit_lambdas per cluster, it's likely that for each cluster we just sum over its point_exit_lambdas (which are the exit lambdas of points that belong to that cluster). So stability = sum(max(0, lambda - birth_lambda) for lambda in point_exit_lambdas). This is straightforward and doesn't require point allocation across hierarchy.\n\nBut then what about the parent relationships and condensation? The min_cluster_size filter: we drop clusters with size < min_cluster_size, and then we need to \"condense\" the hierarchy: i.e., if we drop a cluster, its children should be reassigned to its parent? The problem says \"Condense a supplied hierarchy under a minimum cluster size\". The typical condensed tree keeps clusters >= min_cluster_size, and removes small clusters, connecting their children to the nearest surviving ancestor. Then for each retained cluster, compute stability as described. Then select clusters based on stability ranking and blocking.\n\nIn the payload, c2 has size 2 and min_cluster_size is 3, so c2 should be dropped. Its parent is c0. c1 has size 4, retained. c0 size 7 retained. So after dropping c2, the children of c2 (none in payload) would be reassigned to c0. But c2 has no children. So no effect. However, do we need to recompute the point_exit_lambdas for c0 after removing c2? Probably not; the stability is based on the original point_exit_lambdas for each cluster, but after condensation, the clusters that are retained may have their point_exit_lambdas aggregated from descendants? The description says \"For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points.\" It doesn't specify that we need to move points from dropped clusters to ancestors. Perhaps the \"points\" refer to the points originally assigned to that cluster in the full hierarchy, not after condensation. But since we dropped small clusters, their points are not considered in any retained cluster? That seems odd.\n\nLet's re-read the problem: \"Condensed-tree construction, cluster stability, and flat selection; metric learning is excluded. Validate every required field before computing a result. Drop clusters smaller than the minimum cluster size. For each retained cluster sum max(0, exit_lambda - birth_lambda) over its points. Rank clusters by decreasing stability and cluster ID. Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors. Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\"\n\nThis suggests we first drop small clusters, then compute stability for the remaining clusters (the retained ones). Then we rank them by stability (descending) and then cluster ID (ascending?). Then we perform a selection: iterate over ranked clusters, select if no already selected descendant prevents it, then block its ancestors. The blocking means that once a cluster is selected, all its ancestors are blocked from being selected. Also, if a cluster has a descendant that is already selected, it cannot be selected (because it would be blocked? Actually, \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it\" means that if a descendant is already selected, the cluster is blocked. And then \"then block its ancestors\" means after selecting it, its ancestors become blocked. So it's a typical greedy selection: process clusters in order of decreasing stability; for each, if it's not blocked, select it and block all its ancestors.\n\nFinally, output: \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs.\" So the output should have `clusters` array (retained clusters) with cluster_id, size, stability, and `selected_cluster_ids` sorted list.\n\nNow, to compute stability for retained clusters after filtering small clusters: we need to decide how to handle points from dropped clusters. If we drop a cluster, its points are not part of any retained cluster? But that would undercount stability. In HDBSCAN, the condensed tree recomputes stability by propagating points upward: points from small clusters are treated as noise? Or they are reattached to the nearest surviving ancestor? The standard HDBSCAN does not drop small clusters from the condensed tree; it prunes the tree but points from small clusters are assigned to the parent cluster as noise? Actually, the condensed tree includes only clusters >= min_cluster_size; clusters below are removed, and their points are assigned to the parent cluster as \"noise\" or part of the parent's stability? In the original HDBSCAN algorithm, the condensed tree is built by removing clusters smaller than min_cluster_size, and the points in those removed clusters are then considered as part of the larger cluster's stability? I recall that the stability of a cluster in the condensed tree is the sum of stabilities of its children (which are clusters >= min_cluster_size) plus the \"excess\" stability from points that exit directly from that cluster (i.e., points that are not part of any child cluster). So if you remove a small cluster, you effectively treat its points as if they exit from the parent cluster at their respective lambdas (since they are no longer part of a child). Therefore, the point_exit_lambdas for a cluster might need to be supplemented with the point_exit_lambdas of its dropped descendants. However, the input does not provide explicit point_exit_lambdas for each point; it's per cluster. So if we drop a cluster, we need to move its point_exit_lambdas to its parent.\n\nThus the algorithm:\n1. Build a tree from the parent map.\n2. Identify clusters with size >= min_cluster_size (retained).\n3. For each cluster, compute its \"base\" stability from its own point_exit_lambdas: sum(max(0, lam - birth_lambda)).\n4. However, for retained clusters, they also need to inherit stability from dropped descendant clusters. So we need to propagate contributions upward for clusters that are dropped. Specifically, if a cluster is dropped, then its stability (including its own points and any inherited from its own dropped descendants) should be added to its surviving parent (if any). This is like a post-order traversal: for each cluster, compute total stability contributed upward to surviving ancestors. Alternatively, we can build a condensed tree where only retained clusters remain, and for each retained cluster, its stability is the sum of:\n   - its own base stability (from its point_exit_lambdas)\n   - plus the base stabilities of all descendant clusters that are dropped (and that have no other retained cluster in between).\n5. Then rank and select.\n\nGiven the complexity and that we have only the public fixtures, we need to infer the exact expected behavior. Let's examine the three cases (all identical). They have min_cluster_size=3. Clusters: c0 (size7, birth0.2, exit[0.4,0.6,0.9]), c1 (size4, birth0.5, exit[0.7,1.0,1.2]), c2 (size2, birth0.8, exit[1.1]). parent: c0 no parent, c1 parent c0, c2 parent c0. c2 size 2 <3, so dropped. c0 and c1 retained.\n\nNow compute stability:\nc1 own base: max(0,0.7-0.5)=0.2, (1.0-0.5)=0.5, (1.2-0.5)=0.7 => sum=1.4\nc0 own base: (0.4-0.2)=0.2, (0.6-0.2)=0.4, (0.9-0.2)=0.7 => sum=1.3\nSince c2 is dropped, its own base: (1.1-0.8)=0.3, sum=0.3. Should that be added to c0? Possibly. So c0 total stability = 1.3 + 0.3 = 1.6. Then c1=1.4.\n\nRanking: c0 (1.6) then c1 (1.4). No ties in stability. Check descendants: c0 has descendant c1 (since c1 parent is c0). In selection: first consider c0 (stability 1.6). No selected descendant yet, so select c0. Then block ancestors of c0: none. Then consider c1: c1 has selected ancestor c0 (which is not a descendant, it's an ancestor). Actually, condition is \"no already selected descendant blocks it\". Since c0 is ancestor of c1, not descendant, it does not block c1. So c1 is eligible. But then after selecting c1, we block its ancestors: c0 becomes blocked (but c0 already selected, so no effect). So both selected? That would yield selected_cluster_ids = [\"c0\",\"c1\"] (sorted). However, typical HDBSCAN selection aims to select a set of clusters that are not in an ancestor-descendant relationship. The blocking rule ensures that if you select a cluster, its ancestors are blocked, but descendants are not blocked? Actually, the description: \"Select a cluster only when no already selected descendant blocks it, then block its ancestors.\" So if a descendant is already selected, it blocks the current cluster (i.e., you cannot select an ancestor if a descendant is already selected). But it doesn't say that an ancestor blocks a descendant. So in our order, selecting c0 first does not block c1 (descendant). Then when selecting c1, it blocks c0 (ancestor) but c0 already selected, so fine