sal: for each cluster, if its stability is greater than the sum of stabilities of its children, it is selected, otherwise not. But the description here suggests a global ranking and then selection with blocking. Possibly it's a simplified version.\n\nSince we don't have ground truth, we need to implement exactly as described. So we will do:\n- Filter out clusters with size < min_cluster_size.\n- For the remaining clusters (condensed), compute their total stability as: own base stability + sum of stabilities from dropped descendants (recursively). This requires building the hierarchy and passing up contributions.\n- Rank the retained clusters by descending stability, then ascending cluster_id (string compare).\n- Then perform selection: iterate over ranked list; for each cluster, if none of its descendants (in the *original* tree? or in the condensed tree?) have been selected yet, then select it (add to selected set) and mark all its ancestors as blocked (or mark them as having a selected descendant). Probably we need a \"blocked\" flag per cluster. Implementation: maintain a set `selected` and a set `blocked`. Initialize all false. For each cluster in ranked order:\n   - if cluster is not blocked, then check if any descendant (in the original/condensed tree?) is already selected. If yes, then skip (blocked by descendant). If no, then select cluster, and then for all ancestors of cluster, mark them as blocked (because they have a selected descendant).\n- Return selected_cluster_ids sorted.\n\nNow, we need to define descendant relationships in the original tree? Or in the condensed tree after filtering? Since we drop small clusters, the hierarchy is altered. The parent relationships may need to be updated after dropping small clusters. The descendant relationship should be based on the condensed tree (only retained clusters). Because if we drop c2, its descendant relation to c0 disappears. But c2 is not retained, so it doesn't affect selection. So descendants are only