n (which are either retained or dropped). But careful: For retained clusters, the stability from dropped child clusters should be added, but from retained child clusters, we do not add their stability (they will be separate). So we need to compute: For each cluster (regardless of retained/dropped), we want to compute a value that is the stability that should be assigned to its nearest surviving ancestor. We can do a post-order: for a cluster, compute its own_stability + sum of contributions from children (which are the same). Then if the cluster is retained, that sum becomes its total_stability. If the cluster is dropped, that sum becomes the contribution to its parent (or to the nearest surviving ancestor). But we must ensure that we don't double-count. Simpler: Do a DFS, and for each cluster, compute `combined` = own_stability + sum(combined of children). Then if cluster is retained, set its total_stability = combined. If not retained, we pass up `combined` to its parent (which will eventually be added to a retained cluster). This works.\n8. After computing total_stability for retained clusters, build a list of cluster objects for output: each with cluster_id, size (original size), stability (total_stability).\n9. Rank these clusters by (-stability, cluster_id) (descending stability, ascending cluster_id).\n10. Perform selection:\n    - Maintain a set `selected_ids` and a set `blocked_ids`.\n    - For each cluster in ranked order:\n        - if cluster_id in blocked_ids: skip.\n        - check if any descendant (in condensed tree) is already selected: we need descendant relationships among retained clusters. We'll build a condensed children list for retained clusters. Then we can traverse descendants. To avoid expensive recursion each time, we can precompute a set of descendants for each cluster (or use an ancestor blocking approach). Using a DFS to mark blocked ancestors is efficient. We'll maintain a `selected_flag` and `blocked_flag` per node (using dicts). For each cluster in order:\n            - if blocked_flag[cluster_id] is True: skip.\n            - else, check if any descendant is selected. This can be done by checking the `selected_flag` of all nodes in its subtree. To optimize, we can maintain a `selected_in_subtree` counter or update when selecting. But since number of clusters is small (from input, max few), we can just do a recursive DFS each time.\n            - If no descendant selected, then:\n                - select: set selected_flag=True.\n                - then block all ancestors: traverse parent chain (condensed) and set blocked_flag for each ancestor.\n    - After loop, selected_ids = list of selected cluster_ids, sorted (as required by output: \"sorted selected IDs\").\n11. Output: {\"clusters\": [{\"cluster_id\": ..., \"size\": ..., \"stability\": ...} for each retained cluster (we need to output all retained clusters, not just selected?) The schema says clusters array and selected_cluster_ids array. \"Return all retained stability rows and sorted selected IDs\". So `clusters` should contain all retained clusters with their stability (computed as above). Not just the selected ones. So we output the list of all retained clusters (with cluster_id, size, stability). Then selected_cluster_ids is a sorted list of the selected IDs.\n12. Return the result as a dict (will be automatically JSON-serialized by solve).\n\nEdge case: root cluster with no parent. Annull.\n\nTie handling: \"stable_ties\" says for ties in stability, sort by cluster ID (ascending). The ranking uses that order.\n\nImplementation details in Python:\n- Use standard library: no imports beyond json? Not needed, just return dict.\n- Function `solve(case)` takes a dict (the payload).\n- We'll implement helper functions for tree traversal.\n- Ensure no mutation of input.\n\nLet's write the code.\n\nPseudo:\n\ndef solve(case):\n    min_cluster_size = case['min_cluster_size']\n    clusters_list = case['clusters']\n    parents = case['parent']\n\n    # Build cluster dict\n    clusters = {}\n    for c in clusters_list:\n        cid = c['cluster_id']\n        clusters[cid] = {\n            'size': c['size'],\n            'birth': c['birth_lambda'],\n            'exit_lambdas': c['point_exit_lambdas'],\n            'own_stability': sum(max(0, lam - c['birth_lambda']) for lam in c['point_exit_lambdas'])\n        }\n    # Build children lists\n    children = {cid: [] for cid in clusters}\n    for cid, p in parents.items():\n        if p is not None and p in children:  # p exists in clusters (should)\n            children[p].append(cid)\n\n    # Determine retained clusters\n    retained = {cid for cid, info in clusters.items() if info['size'] >= min_cluster_size}\n\n    # Build condensed parent map for retained clusters (effective parent)\n    condensed_parent = {}\n    for cid in retained:\n        # walk up original parent until find a retained or null\n        cur = cid\n        while True:\n            p = parents.get(cur)\n            if p is None or p in retained:\n                condensed_parent[cid] = p\n                break\n            cur = p\n    # Build condensed children for retained\n    condensed_children = {cid: [] for cid in retained}\n    for cid, p in condensed_parent.items():\n        if p is not None:\n            condensed_children[p].append(cid)\n\n    # Compute total stability for retained\n    # Use post-order recursion on the full tree, but only add contributions to retained.\n    # We'll do a DFS from root(s) - clusters with no parent in original parents map.\n    # Find roots: those in clusters with parent None or parent not in clusters (but payload includes all)\n    roots = [cid for cid in clusters if parents.get(cid) is None]\n\n    total_stability = {}  # only for retained\n    def dfs(cid):\n        info = clusters[cid]\n        total = info['own_stability']\n        for child in children[cid]:\n            total += dfs(child)\n        if cid in retained:\n            total_stability[cid] = total\n        return total\n\n    for root in roots:\n        dfs(root)\n\n    # Output clusters list\n    output_clusters = []\n    for cid in sorted(retained):  # sort for deterministic order? output order not specified, but we can put in arbitrary order; schema is an array, order may not matter.\n        # But we can keep order from input or alphabetical. Let's put in order of original list? Or alphabetical? We'll sort by cluster_id for consistency.\n        output_clusters.append({\n            'cluster_id': cid,\n            'size': clusters[cid]['size'],\n            'stability': total_stability[cid]\n        })\n    # For ranking and selection, we need a list of retained cluster ids possibly not sorted by stability yet.\n    # Create a list of (cid, total_stability) for retained.\n    retained_list = [(cid, total_stability[cid]) for cid in retained]\n    # Rank: sort by (-stability, cid)\n    retained_list.sort(key=lambda x: (-x[1], x[0]))\n    ranked_ids = [cid for cid, _ in retained_list]\n\n    # Selection\n    selected_set = set()\n    blocked_set = set()\n    for cid in ranked_ids:\n        if cid in blocked_set:\n            continue\n        # Check if any descendant in condensed tree is already selected\n        # perform DFS on condensed_children, stopping at selected.\n        def has_selected_descendant(node):\n            if node in selected_set:\n                return True\n            for child in condensed_children.get(node, []):\n                if has_selected_descendant(child):\n                    return True\n            return False\n        if has_selected_descendant(cid):\n            continue\n        # Select\n        selected_set.add(cid)\n        # Block ancestors\n        # Traverse condensed_parent until None\n        cur = cid\n        while True:\n            p = condensed_parent.get(cur)\n            if p is None:\n                break\n            blocked_set.add(p)\n            cur = p\n    # Output sele